Store uploads in Postgres, so one credential is enough

Supabase's Storage API key turned out to be a real obstacle: the dashboard moved
it, the legacy service_role JWT is deprecated, and the new sb_secret_ keys are
not JWTs. Since a Postgres DATABASE_URL was already needed for durable metadata,
the audio can live there too and the second credential disappears entirely.

- New track_files table (audio + artwork as bytea, one row per track, cascaded on
  delete). Blobs sit in their own table so listing the library never loads them.
- Uploads pick a backend automatically: database when Postgres is configured,
  supabase when a bucket is configured, local otherwise.
- /api/stream slices the blob server-side with substr(bytea, from, count), so a
  seek transfers only the requested bytes and still returns 206 + Content-Range.
  /tracks/{id}/artwork serves the artwork column the same way.
- GET /api/health and /api/storage/status report the backend, and durability is
  now simply "not SQLite" — the Settings screen reflects that.
- POST /api/tracks/purge-demo deletes tracks that stream from a remote URL (the
  seeded catalogue) and any playlists left empty. Uploads never have a
  source_url, so they are untouched. SEED_DEMO now defaults to false.

Verified against a real Supabase Postgres: seeded 129 demo tracks, uploaded a
track with cover art, streamed it with a byte range, then deleted the entire
local data/ directory and restarted. The track still listed, streamed (206,
0-4095/1523659) and returned its artwork (200 image/jpeg) — so it genuinely
survives a redeploy. purge-demo then removed the 129 demo tracks and 6 playlists
while keeping the upload.
